{"reply":"One small correction sharpens the connection: in Chapter 8, “The Queen’s Croquet-Ground,” the Queen does discover the mistake. The gardeners explain, “this here ought to have been a _red_ rose-tree, and we put a white one in by mistake,” and when she examines the roses she says, “I see!” before ordering their execution. The scene is not concealment that succeeds; it is an attempt to repair appearances before an authority figure arrives, under an explicitly terrifying threat.

Your note records a quieter version of a related split: you fixed the billing mistake over the weekend, then said nothing on Monday and kept telling yourself it was already handled. That does not tell us why you stayed quiet, but it gives the scene a plausible point of contact: correcting an error and disclosing it are separate acts.

The research is relevant but not diagnostic of your reaction. A 2023 study of hospital nurses found that leader inclusiveness, safety climate, and psychological safety affected willingness to report errors, while fear and practical reporting barriers can inhibit disclosure ([study](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)). Its setting is very different from yours, so it cannot establish that fear caused your silence. It does suggest a useful question: when you think back on the billing mistake, does “already handled” feel more like genuine closure, or like a way of avoiding the additional exposure of saying, “I made the mistake”?","evidence_uses":[{"supported_claims":["In Chapter 8, “The Queen’s Croquet-Ground,” the Queen does discover the mistake.","The gardeners explain, “this here ought to have been a _red_ rose-tree, and we put a white one in by mistake,” and when she examines the roses she says, “I see!” before ordering their execution.","The scene is not concealment that succeeds; it is an attempt to repair appearances before an authority figure arrives, under an explicitly terrifying threat."],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch08-ln1931-1970","source_location":"Chapter 8 — The Queen’s Croquet-Ground, source lines 1931-1970","exact_quote":"“this here ought to have been a _red_ rose-tree, and we put a white one in by mistake;”"},{"supported_claims":["In Chapter 8, “The Queen’s Croquet-Ground,” the Queen does discover the mistake.","The gardeners explain, “this here ought to have been a _red_ rose-tree, and we put a white one in by mistake,” and when she examines the roses she says, “I see!” before ordering their execution.","The scene is not concealment that succeeds; it is an attempt to repair appearances before an authority figure arrives, under an explicitly terrifying threat."],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch08-ln2018-2074","source_location":"Chapter 8 — The Queen’s Croquet-Ground, source li